> **TLDR:** 📦 🛠️ Choose the right event source: Outbox is simple but can lag, Transactional Outbox guarantees atomicity, CDC is real‑time but complex.  

### ⚡️ Problem: “When does my data get out of sync with my events?”  
In a microservice, you often write to a database and want to publish an event to a message broker. If the write succeeds but the publish fails (network glitch, broker down), you end up with a **data‑event split**: the database reflects the change, but downstream services never see it. This inconsistency can cascade, leading to stale caches, incorrect analytics, or failed business flows.  

### 📚 The Three Strategies  

| Pattern | Core Idea | Pros | Cons |
|---------|-----------|------|------|
| **Outbox** | A dedicated “outbox” table is written to alongside the domain entity. A background worker scans the table and publishes events. | • Simple to implement. <br>• No extra DB transaction needed for publish. | • Event may be delayed until worker runs. <br>• Requires separate read‑write concerns. |
| **Transactional Outbox** | The outbox write is part of the same database transaction as the domain write, usually via a trigger or application‑level code. | • Guarantees atomicity: either both writes succeed or none. <br>• No out‑of‑band consistency risk. | • Adds write overhead. <br>• Requires support for write‑ahead logs or triggers. |
| **CDC (Change Data Capture)** | A log‑based system (Debezium, native CDC) streams every DB change to a broker. | • Near‑real‑time event emission. <br>• No additional tables or code. | • Requires database support and extra infrastructure. <br>• Harder to filter or transform events. |

### 🔍 Decision Matrix: When to Pick Each  

| Use‑case | Outbox | Transactional Outbox | CDC |
|----------|--------|----------------------|-----|
| **Low latency, simple services** | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |
| **Strong consistency needed** | ✖ | ✔ | ✖ |
| **Minimal schema changes** | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |
| **High write volume, worry about extra rows** | ✔ | ✖ | ✔ |
| **Existing CDC pipeline** | ✖ | ✖ | ✔ |
| **Need to transform events** | ✔ | ✔ | ✖ (needs downstream transformation) |

### 🛠️ Quick Implementation Sketch (Transactional Outbox with Spring Data JPA)  

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
class Order {
  @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
  String status;
  // …
}

@Entity
@Table(name = "outbox_events")
class OutboxEvent {
  @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
  String aggregateType;
  Long aggregateId;
  String payload; // JSON
  Instant createdAt;
}

@Service
class OrderService {
  @Transactional
  void placeOrder(Order order) {
    orderRepository.save(order); // domain write

    // outbox write in same tx
    OutboxEvent e = new OutboxEvent();
    e.setAggregateType("Order");
    e.setAggregateId(order.getId());
    e.setPayload("{\"orderId\":" + order.getId() + "}");
    e.setCreatedAt(Instant.now());
    outboxRepository.save(e);
  }
}
```

A lightweight worker polls `outbox_events` and publishes to Kafka, then marks the row as sent or deletes it. The key is that the DB transaction guarantees the order write and the outbox row are committed together.

### 🧩 Architecture Kit Diagram  

```architecture
{
  "version": 1,
  "title": "Event Source Strategies",
  "description": "Comparing Outbox, Transactional Outbox, and CDC flows",
  "view": "data-flow",
  "pattern": "reactive-pull",
  "direction": "LR",
  "groups": [
    { "id": "db", "label": "Database", "tone": "slate" },
    { "id": "worker", "label": "Event Worker / CDC Agent", "tone": "green" },
    { "id": "broker", "label": "Message Broker (Kafka)", "tone": "blue" }
  ],
  "nodes": [
    { "id": "orderSvc", "component": "order-service", "label": "Order Service", "group": "db" },
    { "id": "outboxTbl", "component": "outbox-table", "label": "Outbox Table", "group": "db" },
    { "id": "cdcAgent", "component": "cdc-agent", "label": "CDC Agent", "group": "worker" },
    { "id": "outboxWorker", "component": "outbox-worker", "label": "Outbox Worker", "group": "worker" },
    { "id": "kafka", "component": "kafka-topic", "label": "Kafka Topic", "group": "broker" }
  ],
  "edges": [
    { "from": "orderSvc", "to": "outboxTbl", "kind": "write" },
    { "from": "outboxTbl", "to": "outboxWorker", "kind": "poll" },
    { "from": "outboxWorker", "to": "kafka", "kind": "publish" },
    { "from": "orderSvc", "to": "cdcAgent", "kind": "write" },
    { "from": "cdcAgent", "to": "kafka", "kind": "publish" }
  ],
  "takeaway": { "title": "Takeaway", "body": "Transactional Outbox gives atomicity; Outbox is simple; CDC is fastest but needs infra." }
}
```

### ✅ Takeaway  
- **Transactional Outbox** is the gold‑standard for strong consistency.  
- **Outbox** is great for simpler, low‑latency needs.  
- **CDC** shines when you already have a log‑based pipeline and need near‑real‑time events.  
Pick the pattern that aligns with your consistency requirements, latency tolerance, and infrastructure maturity.

